[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc16
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the application/service layer on PC16 — specifically the OpenSSH server (sshd), which the users rely on for file sharing (scp/rsync/SFTP).

Mechanism: With sshd stopped on PC16, TCP connection attempts to port 22 from PC15 time out/refuse, giving the impression that “the other machine does not answer,” even though basic IP connectivity is fine. ICMP pings still work across the L2 segment, so the network path and switch are healthy.

Localization: PC16’s ssh service/process. Evidence: systemctl on PC16 showed “ssh.service … Active: inactive (dead)” and netstat showed no listener on :22. After starting the service, netstat shows 0.0.0.0:22 and :::22 in LISTEN, confirming restoration. Additionally, PC15 <-> PC16 pings are sub-millisecond in both directions, confirming Layer 2/3 are OK.

Root cause: The SSH server on PC16 was not running (sshd inactive), so PC16 was not answering file-sharing connections.

Corrective fix: I started the ssh service on PC16 (systemctl start ssh). Verified it is now listening on TCP/22. File sharing to PC16 (e.g., scp/rsync/SFTP) should work again from PC15. Note: PC15 currently has no SSH listener either (no :22 in netstat), so reverse-direction SSH to PC15 will still fail unless you also enable ssh there (systemctl enable --now ssh) if bidirectional sharing is required. No switch, cabling, or IP configuration changes are needed; the network is healthy.
=== END ===